@host = http://localhost:5185
@assetId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001
@licenseeId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000099
@ownerId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010

### ─── Rights Profile ─────────────────────────────────────────────

### 1. Create a rights profile for an asset
# ⚠️ releaseStatus is a raw string — "Banana" would be accepted without error.
# In the DDD API, releaseStatus is a validated [Flags] enum.
PUT {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/rights-profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "ownerId": "{{ownerId}}",
  "releaseStatus": "ModelRelease"
}

### 2. Get the rights profile
GET {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/rights-profile

### 3. Add a restriction: editorial only (block commercial)
# ⚠️ restrictedPurpose is a raw string — "Commercal" typo silently fails.
# In the DDD API, UsagePurpose is an enum validated at compile time.
POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/rights-profile/restrictions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "description": "Editorial use only - commercial blocked",
  "restrictedPurpose": "Commercial"
}

### 4. Add a restriction: no broadcast in US
# ⚠️ Territory codes are joined into a comma-separated string internally.
# No ISO code validation — "ZZ" would be accepted.
POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/rights-profile/restrictions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "description": "No broadcast in US",
  "restrictedChannel": "Broadcast",
  "restrictedTerritoryCodes": ["US"]
}

### ─── License Requests ───────────────────────────────────────────

### 5. Scenario A: Allowed editorial use (Web + Norway + 3 months)
# Expected: isAllowed = true, grantId returned
POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-requests
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "licenseeId": "{{licenseeId}}",
  "channel": "Web",
  "territoryCodes": ["NO"],
  "timeWindowStart": "2026-03-01T00:00:00Z",
  "timeWindowEnd": "2026-06-01T00:00:00Z",
  "purpose": "Editorial",
  "isExclusive": false
}

### 6. Scenario B: Denied commercial use
# Expected: isAllowed = false, denialReasons contains "commercial blocked"
POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-requests
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "licenseeId": "{{licenseeId}}",
  "channel": "Web",
  "territoryCodes": ["NO"],
  "timeWindowStart": "2026-03-01T00:00:00Z",
  "timeWindowEnd": "2026-06-01T00:00:00Z",
  "purpose": "Commercial",
  "isExclusive": false
}

### 7. Scenario C: First exclusive license request (should succeed)
# Expected: isAllowed = true, exclusive scope reserved
POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-requests
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "licenseeId": "{{licenseeId}}",
  "channel": "Web",
  "territoryCodes": ["NO"],
  "timeWindowStart": "2026-03-01T00:00:00Z",
  "timeWindowEnd": "2026-06-01T00:00:00Z",
  "purpose": "Editorial",
  "isExclusive": true
}

### 8. Scenario C: Second exclusive request (should be DENIED — ExclusiveConflict)
# Run after step 7. Same scope → conflict.
POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-requests
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "licenseeId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000088",
  "channel": "Web",
  "territoryCodes": ["NO"],
  "timeWindowStart": "2026-03-01T00:00:00Z",
  "timeWindowEnd": "2026-06-01T00:00:00Z",
  "purpose": "Editorial",
  "isExclusive": true
}

### ─── License Grants ─────────────────────────────────────────────

### 9. List all grants for an asset
GET {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-grants

### 10. List active-only grants for an asset
GET {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-grants?activeOnly=true

### 11. Get a specific grant (replace with actual grantId from step 5 or 7)
# GET {{host}}/license-grants/<grantId>

### 12. Scenario D: Revoke a grant (replace with actual grantId)
# POST {{host}}/license-grants/<grantId>/revoke
# Content-Type: application/json
#
# {
#   "reason": "Contract breach - unauthorized sublicensing",
#   "revokedBy": "admin@example.com"
# }

### ─── Exclusive Windows ──────────────────────────────────────────

### 13. Add an exclusive window manually (replace grantId)
# POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/rights-profile/exclusive-windows
# Content-Type: application/json
#
# {
#   "grantId": "<grantId>",
#   "channel": "Web",
#   "territoryCodes": ["NO"],
#   "timeWindowStart": "2026-03-01T00:00:00Z",
#   "timeWindowEnd": "2026-06-01T00:00:00Z",
#   "purpose": "Editorial"
# }

### ─── Scattered Validation Demo ──────────────────────────────────
# This section demonstrates the "scattered validation" anti-pattern.
# A second developer added a bulk-revoke endpoint that bypasses LicenseService.
#
# Run steps 1–7 first (create profile, add restrictions, issue an exclusive grant).
# Then follow these steps to see the problem:

### 14. Scenario E: Bulk-revoke bypasses validation
# ⚠️ This endpoint goes directly to the data store — no guards, no audit trail, no cleanup.
# Compare to step 12 which uses LicenseService.RevokeLicense() correctly.
POST {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-grants/bulk-revoke
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "Admin cleanup",
  "revokedBy": "admin@example.com"
}

### 15. Check the damage — grants show "Revoked" but audit fields are empty
# After step 14, GET a grant and notice:
#   - RevocationReason is NULL (not set!)
#   - RevokedBy is NULL
#   - RevokedAt is NULL
#   - No audit trail at all
GET {{host}}/assets/{{assetId}}/license-grants

### 16. Check the damage — exclusive windows are still there!
# After bulk-revoke, the exclusive window was NOT cleaned up.
# So a NEW exclusive license request will be WRONGLY DENIED with ExclusiveConflict,
# even though the original grant was revoked.
# Run step 7 again after bulk-revoke to see the phantom conflict.

### 17. Try the same thing with the proper revoke endpoint (step 12)
# The LicenseService.RevokeLicense() method:
#   ✅ Checks "cannot revoke expired"
#   ✅ Checks "already revoked"
#   ✅ Sets RevocationReason, RevokedBy, RevokedAt
#   ✅ Removes exclusive windows for the grant
#
# But nothing stops a developer from bypassing it — the entity has public setters.
